<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ali Dinar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1916">1916</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ali <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinar">Dinar</a> was the last sultan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur Sultanate</a>, who restored the sultanate in 1898 after the fall of the Mahdist state. He ruled with considerable independence under nominal recognition of Anglo-Egyptian authority. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he sided with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and he was killed in 1916 after a British expedition conquered Darfur.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ali-dinar.jpg" alt="Ali Dinar" width="320" height="146" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: British military photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ali_Dinar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Muhammad Ali Dinar, born around 1865, belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keira-dynasty">Keira dynasty</a>. He first took the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a> in 1891, after his cousin, the shadow sultan Abū l-Khayrāt, was killed by a band of rebellious slaves; some sources allege that Ali Dinar had incited the killing, which he denied. His position was weak. Urged by his followers to negotiate with the Mahdists in al-Fāshir, he was raided by the Masālīt and despoiled by Bakhīt Abū Risha of Dār Silā, and on 13 October 1891 he surrendered in Mahdist-occupied al-Fāshir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At first well received, he was later imprisoned for drinking wine and saved only by the intercession of the Mahdist governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a>, Maḥmūd w. Aḥmad, whose entourage he joined. With him he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omdurman">Omdurman</a>, the Mahdist capital, and met the Khalīfa 'Abdallāhi. He went back to Darfur in June 1894, but suspicion that he meant to rebel had him sent back to Omdurman in January 1895. There he is said to have served as an attendant of the Khalīfa or his son and to have known Rudolf Slatin, and in 1896 he fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuba-mountains">Nuba Mountains</a> under the commander Ibrāhīm al-Khalīl.</p><p>Just before or during the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omdurman">Omdurman</a> he fled back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a> with many notables of the old sultanate. He overcame the Mahdist garrison at al-Fāshir and rival claimants and quickly reasserted the sultanate's authority, working above all to set up a functioning judiciary and to root out Mahdist influence. He also opened diplomatic ties with neighbours such as the Sānūsiyya, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadai-empire">Wadai</a>, Dār Silā and Dār al-Kūtī.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1915, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, Ali Dinar declared his support for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. The British government answered with an invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a>, and he was killed in action on 6 November 1916. His sultanate was then incorporated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-egyptian-condominium">Anglo-Egyptian Sudan</a>, and he was the last sultan of Darfur.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ali Dinar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Likewise, <b>Ali Dinar</b>'s restored sultanate, though it possessed riflemen, had no modern artillery or machine guns to match the Anglo-Egyptian expeditionary force, supported by aircraft, that defeated his army at the Battle of Birinjiya near El Fasher in May 1916.</blockquote><a href="/empire/darfur-sultanate">Darfur Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The sultans ruled a farming people from fortified centres, balancing between the Mahdist State, the Wadai Empire and the restored Darfur Sultanate of <b>Ali Dinar</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/dar-masalit">Dar Masalit</a></li></ul></section>
